problemStatement:
A developer wrote CSS for a two‑column layout but the columns don't fit side by side. Explain why and fix it.

HTML:
```html
<div class="row">
  <div class="col">Column 1</div>
  <div class="col">Column 2</div>
</div>
```

CSS (flawed):
```css
.row {
  width: 600px;
  background: #eee;
}
.col {
  width: 50%;
  padding: 10px;
  border: 1px solid #999;
  float: left;
}
```

**Problems & Questions:**
1. Why don't the two 50% columns fit inside the 600px row? (Explain using the box model).
2. What is the total width of each column in `content-box`?
3. Rewrite the CSS to make the columns fit exactly inside the row **without changing the visual spacing** (i.e., they still have 10px padding and 1px border). Use `box-sizing: border-box;` either globally or on the columns.
4. Show the corrected CSS.

Also, include a comment block at the top that answers question 1 and 2.

Provide the final CSS.

solutionCode:
/* 1. With content-box, each .col has content width 300px (50% of 600). 
   Plus 10px left/right padding (20px) and 1px left/right border (2px) = 322px total. 
   Two columns = 644px, which is wider than the 600px row, causing them to wrap.
2. Total width per column = 322px. */

/* Corrected CSS with border-box */
*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.row {
  width: 600px;
  background: #eee;
}

.col {
  width: 50%;
  padding: 10px;
  border: 1px solid #999;
  float: left;
}
/* Now 50% includes padding and border, each column exactly 300px total, fitting perfectly. */
